// cases_event.cla: EventXRec (Task 6, toolbox-integration Feature B) --
// exercises `extern record` native storage/decay (EXRecAddr, CvSExt16)
// against a REAL Toolbox trap, inside a booted program: cg68k's own
// listing golden (testdata/cg68k/xrec.cla) proves the emitted instructions
// look right; this case proves the SAME codegen dispatches correctly
// against real ROM, the same "user-code externs are the proven pattern"
// precedent cases_events.cla/cases_gestalt.cla/cases_a5.cla already
// establish. Point/EventRecord used to be declared LOCALLY here (not
// reused from runtime/clarus/ui.cla) for the identical reason; as of
// toolbox-cookbook Task 2 they instead come from toolbox/events.cla
// (that catalog's own copies, composed into this same build) -- extern
// records have no dedup rule, so keeping a second local copy here would
// collide with the catalog's.
//
// Trap-word convention (task-6-review Critical 1 fix -- the FIRST version
// of this file declared `TbOSEventAvail = trap 0xA030` as plain Pascal,
// which is WRONG): the trap word's own bit 11 (0x0800) is Apple's own
// OS-vs-Toolbox dispatch-convention flag -- CLEAR means an OS trap using
// REGISTER convention (args in D0/A0, result in D0, NO stack involvement:
// the caller pushes nothing and the trap dispatcher pops nothing); SET
// means a Toolbox trap using PASCAL convention (args pushed by the
// caller, popped by the trap dispatcher before it returns). This is the
// trap-table reading rule: `trapWord & 0x0800` -- zero is OS/register,
// nonzero is Toolbox/Pascal.
//
// `_OSEventAvail` is 0xA030 -- `0xA030 & 0x0800 == 0` -- an OS trap,
// REGISTER convention, despite Inside Macintosh listing a Pascal-looking
// `FUNCTION OSEventAvail (eventMask: INTEGER; VAR theEvent: EventRecord):
// BOOLEAN;` signature: that signature describes the LANGUAGE-level call
// shape a Pascal/C glue function generates around the raw trap, not the
// raw trap-dispatch convention itself -- the two differ for OS traps
// specifically (`_FlushEvents`, 0xA032, right next to it in the same OS
// Event Manager family, is bit-11-clear too; `runtime/clarus/ui.cla`'s own
// `UiFlushEvents = trap 0xA032` plain-Pascal declaration is the SAME
// latent mis-declaration this file's first draft copied -- OUT of this
// task's scope, ledgered as a follow-up, not fixed here).
//
// Fixed by using the real Toolbox EVENT MANAGER sibling instead:
// `_EventAvail`, 0xA971 -- one trap word after `_GetNextEvent`'s own
// 0xA970 -- `0xA971 & 0x0800 != 0` (bit 11 SET), a genuine Pascal-
// convention trap, matching `ui.cla`'s own already-correct
// `UiGetNextEvent = trap 0xA970` / `UiWaitNextEvent = trap 0xA860`
// (both also bit-11-SET). EventAvail's signature and semantics are the
// SAME check-without-removing-from-the-queue query OSEventAvail performs
// (Inside Macintosh, Event Manager) -- only the calling convention
// differs, and this is the one this file's plain `= trap` clause (no
// `reg`) actually matches.
external func TbEventAvail(mask: word, ev: ptr): bool = trap 0xA971

// TbXRecAddr: clause-less extern -> nat_TbXRecAddr fallback (verified
// against testdata/cg68k/traps.cla's own TCustomOp/nat_TCustomOp pair,
// the established "no trap clause -> nat_<Name> Clarus function"
// convention, cgCallExtNatFallback/cg68k.cla) -- the native-lane sibling
// of Task 5's own rt_ext_XRecAddr host-lane identity shim: "the address of
// my own argument" has no real Toolbox trap behind it, so both lanes
// resolve it as a plain identity instead.
external func TbXRecAddr(p: ptr): ptr

func nat_TbXRecAddr(p: ptr): ptr {
    return p
}

// caseEventXRec: fills ev with a nonzero sentinel (so a marshaling bug
// that leaves ev untouched fails closed, not a false pass from an
// already-zero record), calls the real EventAvail trap, then cross-checks
// TWO independent read paths of the SAME resulting bytes: a raw peek
// through TbXRecAddr(ev)'s own computed address (offsets 0/2/6/10 --
// what/message/when/where.v, Task 4's own verified packing anchors)
// against the corresponding ordinary field read (`ev.what`/`ev.message`/
// `ev.when`/`ev.where.v`, which routes through CvSExt16 for the two word
// fields). message/when are full 32-bit fields (peekl and a field read
// are numerically identical, sign irrelevant); what/where.v are word
// fields but both expected values stay well under the 16-bit sign
// boundary in practice (mouse coordinates, small event codes) so a raw
// zero-extending peekw and a sign-extending field read agree numerically
// either way. A wrong TbXRecAddr/EXRecAddr offset computation would read
// from the wrong address and almost certainly disagree with the field
// read of the SAME logical field.
//
// NOT asserted: "no event is pending" (`ev.what == 0`). This suite's own
// GUI just opened a window and fired a real button click before this
// case runs (testdata/ui/toolboxsuite.events) -- the OS genuinely queues
// a real event (an activateEvt, code 8, observed) across that, so
// EventAvail correctly reports TRUE with a nonzero `what` here; asserting
// "always false" would be asserting a specific idle OS queue state this
// suite's own scripted interaction doesn't provide, not testing the
// extern-record codegen. Instead: `got == (ev.what != 0)` is EventAvail's
// own documented contract (Inside Macintosh: returns TRUE exactly when
// theEvent's `what` comes back nonzero) -- environment-independent, and
// still the convention gate Critical 1 wants: a register-vs-pascal
// mismatch leaves the stack unbalanced and `got` holding whatever garbage
// the mismatched pop left in D0, which will not coincidentally satisfy
// this exact self-consistency relation (nor agree with the four
// independent peek/field cross-checks) across BOTH native lanes.
func caseEventXRec(): TestResult {
    var ev: EventRecord
    var got: bool
    var wantGot: bool
    var base: ptr
    var w0: int
    var l2: int
    var l6: int
    var w10: int
    var detail: string

    ev.what = -1
    ev.message = -1
    ev.when = -1
    ev.where.v = -1
    ev.where.h = -1
    ev.modifiers = -1

    got = TbEventAvail(0xFFFF, ev)
    wantGot = ev.what != 0

    base = TbXRecAddr(ev)
    w0 = peekw(base + 0)
    l2 = peekl(base + 2)
    l6 = peekl(base + 6)
    w10 = peekw(base + 10)

    if got == wantGot and w0 == ev.what and l2 == ev.message and l6 == ev.when and w10 == ev.where.v {
        return tkPass("EventXRec")
    }
    // Built up piecewise into `detail`, same native-68k temp-slot-budget
    // reason as cases_events.cla's own cases.
    if got {
        detail = "got true"
    } else {
        detail = "got false"
    }
    detail = detail + " what " + tkIntToStr(ev.what)
    detail = detail + " w0 " + tkIntToStr(w0)
    detail = detail + " message " + tkIntToStr(ev.message)
    detail = detail + " l2 " + tkIntToStr(l2)
    detail = detail + " when " + tkIntToStr(ev.when)
    detail = detail + " l6 " + tkIntToStr(l6)
    detail = detail + " where.v " + tkIntToStr(ev.where.v)
    detail = detail + " w10 " + tkIntToStr(w10)
    return tkFail("EventXRec", detail)
}
